1
00:00:01,127 --> 00:00:09,010
Welcome to Great Britain's regions, part 1 of 1. First, I clear the chosen
names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09,670 --> 00:00:21,524
We work through 15 of the Great Britain regions the map names, once each:
where it sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:22,234 --> 00:00:25,647
Let's bring Highlands and Islands into view.

4
00:00:30,126 --> 00:00:45,318
The Highlands and Islands take up the far north of Great Britain, the glens
and mountains of the Scottish mainland above the Highland line together with
the Western Isles, Orkney and Shetland, with Inverness as the largest town.

5
00:00:45,976 --> 00:01:01,417
Ben Nevis here is the highest mountain in Britain at 1,345 metres, and the
Highland Clearances emptied many of these glens of people so that sheep could
graze them between 1750 and 1860.

6
00:01:02,082 --> 00:01:09,118
Remember the roof of Britain, where the glens were emptied for sheep and
Gaelic is still spoken every day.

7
00:01:09,807 --> 00:01:12,663
Let's bring North Eastern into view.

8
00:01:17,581 --> 00:01:29,261
North Eastern is the far north-east corner of Scotland, facing the North Sea
and the Moray Firth, north of the Eastern region, with Aberdeen on the coast.

9
00:01:29,926 --> 00:01:40,027
Aberdeen here is called the Granite City, because it was built almost entirely
from grey stone quarried at Rubislaw, once the biggest man-made hole in
Europe.

10
00:01:40,692 --> 00:01:50,026
Remember the granite city on the North Sea, where oil from under the seabed
turned a fishing port into an offshore capital.

11
00:01:50,724 --> 00:01:53,104
Let's bring Eastern into view.

12
00:01:57,618 --> 00:02:13,386
Eastern is the belt of Scotland along the North Sea, between the Highlands in
the north and the English border in the south, holding Edinburgh and the old
kingdom of Fife, and it touches five other regions on this map.

13
00:02:14,039 --> 00:02:26,624
Edinburgh here climbs a volcanic ridge in its old town and spreads in a grid
below it in the new town, laid out in 1767, and the two together are a world
heritage site.

14
00:02:27,276 --> 00:02:33,580
Remember the city on the dead volcano, with the Forth Bridge beside it whose
painting was never finished.

15
00:02:34,270 --> 00:02:36,522
Let's bring South Western into view.

16
00:02:41,171 --> 00:02:51,272
South Western is Scotland’s south-west corner, on the Irish Sea and the Firth
of Clyde, with Glasgow as its largest city and the Highlands rising to the
north.

17
00:02:51,929 --> 00:03:02,808
The Clyde shipyards here built a fifth of the world’s ships by 1900, and later
the ocean liners Queen Mary and Queen Elizabeth.

18
00:03:03,458 --> 00:03:09,809
Remember the green hollow of the Clyde, the name behind Glasgow, where the
great liners were launched.

19
00:03:10,501 --> 00:03:13,520
Let's bring North East into view.

20
00:03:17,180 --> 00:03:29,162
North East is the wedge of north-east England between the Pennines and the
North Sea, running from the Scottish border down to the Tees, with Newcastle
as its largest city.

21
00:03:29,824 --> 00:03:42,955
This was Northumbria, the land north of the Humber, a kingdom that once
reached from the Humber up to Edinburgh, and Hadrian’s Wall crosses the
region, begun in the year 122.

22
00:03:43,610 --> 00:03:51,493
Remember the Roman wall running across the region to the sea, with the wild
coast and its island monastery beyond.

23
00:03:52,181 --> 00:03:54,526
Let's bring North West into view.

24
00:03:59,104 --> 00:04:12,757
North West covers England’s north-west corner, from the Irish Sea and the Lake
District up to the Scottish border, with Manchester as its largest city, and
on this map it touches seven other regions.

25
00:04:13,415 --> 00:04:26,639
Manchester here was the first industrial city in the world, and cotton made
it; the Lake District in the same region is England’s largest national park,
made famous by Wordsworth and Beatrix Potter.

26
00:04:27,288 --> 00:04:33,639
Remember the cotton city with the fells behind it, and the lake poets who
wrote about those hills.

27
00:04:34,332 --> 00:04:38,128
Let's bring Yorkshire and the Humber into view.

28
00:04:41,467 --> 00:04:51,277
Yorkshire and the Humber is the east-central region of England, from the
Pennines across to the North Sea and the Humber estuary, with Leeds as its
largest city.

29
00:04:51,931 --> 00:05:03,274
Yorkshire was once the largest English county and was split into three
Ridings, from the Norse word for a third, which is why there was never a South
Riding.

30
00:05:03,938 --> 00:05:12,889
Remember the county too big to be one county, split into three Ridings, with
York its Viking capital for almost a century.

31
00:05:13,602 --> 00:05:16,028
Let's bring East Midlands into view.

32
00:05:21,504 --> 00:05:30,931
East Midlands is the middle land of England, running from the hills of
Derbyshire and Nottingham in the west across to the flat Lincolnshire coast on
the North Sea.

33
00:05:31,592 --> 00:05:42,726
This was the core of Mercia, the kingdom that dominated the 700s, and the
Danelaw’s Five Boroughs were all here, which is why Norse words survive in the
local dialect.

34
00:05:43,386 --> 00:05:49,986
Remember the middle land of Mercia, with Sherwood Forest and the Robin Hood
story in its eastern woods.

35
00:05:50,662 --> 00:05:52,624
Let's bring West Midlands into view.

36
00:05:57,878 --> 00:06:08,501
West Midlands is the inland half of the Midlands, between Wales and the East
Midlands with no coast of its own, and Birmingham as its largest city.

37
00:06:09,165 --> 00:06:19,359
Birmingham here had more canal miles than Venice at its peak, all built to
haul coal and iron out of the Black Country, whose name comes from the soot
and the thick coal seam beneath it.

38
00:06:20,016 --> 00:06:27,899
Remember the black country and its canals, with Shakespeare’s birthplace at
Stratford upon Avon down by the river.

39
00:06:28,593 --> 00:06:30,845
Let's bring East Wales into view.

40
00:06:35,571 --> 00:06:48,284
East Wales is the part of Wales that meets England, running from the Bristol
Channel coast in the south up along the English boundary to the northern
coast, with Swansea as its largest city.

41
00:06:48,939 --> 00:06:57,635
Offa’s Dyke, the earthwork Mercia raised along this border in the eighth
century, can still be walked for 285 kilometres.

42
00:06:58,295 --> 00:07:07,757
Remember the old dyke still walking the Welsh border, and the two names for
one people, Wealas to the English and Cymru to the Welsh.

43
00:07:08,444 --> 00:07:12,322
Let's bring West Wales and the Valleys into view.

44
00:07:15,696 --> 00:07:26,273
West Wales and the Valleys takes the western and southern side of Wales, the
narrow valleys between the mountains and the sea, with Cardiff on the coast as
its largest city.

45
00:07:26,927 --> 00:07:43,019
The Valleys are the narrow coal valleys of the south, and Wales once had over
600 working pits in 1913, with the last deep mine, Tower Colliery, bought by
its own miners and running until 2008.

46
00:07:43,675 --> 00:07:51,860
Remember the valleys of coal with slate roofs over the mountains, and the last
pit run by the men who bought it.

47
00:07:52,540 --> 00:07:54,665
Let's bring East into view.

48
00:07:59,247 --> 00:08:10,416
East is England’s easternmost region, the bulge of East Anglia on the North
Sea between the Wash and the Thames estuary, with Southend-on-Sea as its
largest city.

49
00:08:11,072 --> 00:08:20,453
The Fens were drained by Dutch engineers from the 1630s, and they are now the
flattest and most fertile land in Britain, with some of it below sea level.

50
00:08:21,117 --> 00:08:29,906
Remember the flat reclaimed fenland and its drainage ditches, with the warrior
helmet from the ship burial at Sutton Hoo.

51
00:08:30,586 --> 00:08:33,314
Let's bring Greater London into view.

52
00:08:37,317 --> 00:08:47,766
Greater London is a small region set on its own inside the south-east corner
of England, ringed by the South East and the East region, and the map names it
separately.

53
00:08:48,427 --> 00:09:06,515
It was the largest city in the world from about 1825 to 1925, and the Great
Fire of 1666 destroyed 13,200 houses and 87 churches, of which Wren rebuilt
51.

54
00:09:07,169 --> 00:09:15,435
Remember the tiny separate patch in the south-east corner, holding the city
that was the biggest in the world for a century.

55
00:09:16,122 --> 00:09:18,630
Let's bring South East into view.

56
00:09:23,048 --> 00:09:33,369
South East is the ring of country around London, from the Thames estuary and
the Channel coast up to the Chiltern hills, with Brighton as its largest city.

57
00:09:34,027 --> 00:09:43,837
The Channel is 33 kilometres wide at Dover, and the tunnel under it, opened in
1994, has the longest undersea section of any rail tunnel.

58
00:09:44,491 --> 00:09:54,464
Remember the narrowest crossing to France at Dover, with Canterbury, the seat
of English Christianity since 597, in the same region.

59
00:09:55,152 --> 00:09:57,404
Let's bring South West into view.

60
00:10:01,919 --> 00:10:12,496
South West is England’s far south-west corner, from the Dorset coast and the
Bristol Channel up to Bristol, with Cornwall and Devon at its western tip.

61
00:10:13,160 --> 00:10:26,674
Stonehenge and Bath are both here, the stones raised around 2500 BC, and
Bath’s spring has poured hot water at 46 degrees Celsius since long before
Rome plumbed it.

62
00:10:27,328 --> 00:10:36,453
Remember the standing stones and the hot spring, with the tin coast of
Cornwall and its name meaning the foreigners of Kernow beyond.

63
00:10:42,088 --> 00:11:02,824
15 regions of Great Britain, one for each target of the map: Highlands and
Islands, North Eastern, Eastern, South Western, North East, North West,
Yorkshire and the Humber, East Midlands, West Midlands, East Wales, West Wales
and the Valleys, East, Greater London, South East and South West.

64
00:11:03,483 --> 00:11:13,851
Remember the shape of Great Britain from the inside: the regions nest around
each other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
